No. In C++ (and most languages using IEEE 754 double-precision floats), `0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3` evaluates to `false`.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <iomanip>

int main() {
    double a = 0.1 + 0.2;
    double b = 0.3;
    std::cout << std::boolalpha << (a == b) << "\n"; // false
    std::cout << std::setprecision(20) << a << "\n";  // 0.30000000000000004441
    std::cout << std::setprecision(20) << b << "\n";  // 0.29999999999999998890
}
```

The issue is that 0.1, 0.2, and 0.3 can't be represented exactly in binary floating point, so each gets rounded to the nearest representable double. The rounding errors for 0.1 and 0.2 don't cancel out to exactly match the rounding error for 0.3.

The standard fix is to compare with a tolerance instead of exact equality:

```cpp
#include <cmath>

bool nearlyEqual(double a, double b, double epsilon = 1e-9) {
    return std::fabs(a - b) < epsilon;
}
```

This is relevant in your `simplify_geometry.h` / `dots.cpp` work too if you're ever comparing computed distances or coordinates for equality. Worth checking if any of that code does `==` on floating-point results rather than an epsilon comparison.

